<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd"><key id="d6" for="edge" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d5" for="edge" attr.name="keywords" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d4" for="edge" attr.name="description" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d3" for="edge" attr.name="weight" attr.type="double"/>
<key id="d2" for="node" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d1" for="node" attr.name="description" attr.type="string"/>
<key id="d0" for="node" attr.name="entity_type" attr.type="string"/>
<graph edgedefault="undirected"><node id="&quot;ROM MOTION TRACKING RULES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A set of guidelines for using AI to track range of motion (ROM) in telehealth physical therapy (PT)."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AI-BASED RULES FOR TRACKING BODY MOVEMENT IN TELEHEALTH PT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A subset of ROM Motion Tracking Rules, these guidelines focus on using AI technologies to monitor body movements in a telehealth setting."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OPENPOSE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A computer vision tool used to track joint angles in real-time for ROM tracking."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAPIPE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A computer vision tool used to track joint angles in real-time for ROM tracking."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IMU SENSORS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Inertial Measurement Unit sensors used to track joint angles in real-time for ROM tracking."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BONY LANDMARKS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Specific anatomical points used as reference points for tracking joint movements (e.g., acromion for the shoulder, greater trochanter for the hip)."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MOVEMENT PLANES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Planes of movement defined for tracking joint angles relative to anatomical neutral (sagittal, frontal, transverse)."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ABNORMAL MOVEMENT PATTERNS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Patterns of movement that deviate from normal, such as lumbar extension during shoulder flexion, which can indicate compensation or injury."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BILATERAL SYMMETRY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The analysis of symmetry between the left and right sides of the body to detect injuries or imbalances."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CERVICAL SPINE (NECK)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The part of the spine in the neck region, with specific ROM guidelines for flexion, extension, lateral flexion, and rotation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SHOULDER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The shoulder joint, with specific ROM guidelines for flexion, extension, abduction, external rotation, and internal rotation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELBOW&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The elbow joint, with specific ROM guidelines for flexion, extension, supination, and pronation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WRIST&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The wrist joint, with specific ROM guidelines for flexion, extension, radial deviation, and ulnar deviation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;THORACIC SPINE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The part of the spine in the chest region, with specific ROM guidelines for flexion, extension, rotation, and lateral flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LUMBAR SPINE (LOWER BACK)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The part of the spine in the lower back region, with specific ROM guidelines for flexion, extension, lateral flexion, and rotation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The hip is a joint that connects the thigh bone (femur) to the pelvis, allowing for a range of movements including flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and rotation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hip joint, with specific ROM guidelines for flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, internal rotation, and external rotation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee is a journal that published a study on the digital goniometric measurement of knee joint motion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The knee is a complex joint in the human body, responsible for a wide range of movements including flexion, extension, and rotation. It involves various anatomical structures such as the femur, tibia, and patella."&lt;SEP&gt;"The knee is a joint in the human body, and its range of motion (ROM) is often measured using tools like the Universal Goniometer (UG)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The knee is a joint in the lower extremity, involving various anatomical structures such as the femur, tibia, and patella. It is crucial for movement and stability in the lower body."&lt;SEP&gt;"The knee is a joint that connects the thigh bone (femur) to the shin bones (tibia and fibula), allowing for flexion and extension movements."&lt;SEP&gt;"The knee joint, with specific ROM guidelines for flexion, extension, internal rotation, and external rotation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56ba6457f7a7f43d803a693f91176eca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ANKLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The ankle joint, with specific ROM guidelines for dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, inversion, and eversion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AI-BASED COMPENSATION DETECTION RULES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Guidelines for identifying abnormal movement patterns that indicate compensation or injury, such as shoulder flexion compensation, lumbar flexion in squatting, and knee valgus in squats/lunges."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GAIT ANALYSIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The analysis of walking patterns to identify compensations or abnormalities, such as limping, hip hike, foot dragging, and circumduction."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE PAIN COMPENSATION DETECTION RULES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Specific guidelines for detecting compensations due to knee pain, including gait analysis and squat/sit-to-stand movements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SCAPULA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The scapula (shoulder blade) is a bone that moves during shoulder flexion, and its movement is monitored to detect compensations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROTATOR CUFF WEAKNESS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the rotator cuff muscles are weak, leading to early excessive scapular elevation during shoulder flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CORE WEAKNESS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the core muscles are weak, leading to early lumbar rounding during squatting."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TIGHT HAMSTRINGS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the hamstring muscles are tight, leading to early lumbar rounding during squatting."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WEAK GLUTE MEDIUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the glute medius muscle is weak, leading to medial knee collapse during squats and lunges."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ACL RISK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The risk of anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) injury due to medial knee collapse during squats and lunges."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ANTALGIC GAIT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A walking pattern characterized by reduced stance phase on the affected leg, often seen in individuals with knee pain."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antalgic Gait is a shorter stride to avoid lumbar compression, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP HIKE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An abnormal gait pattern where the pelvis on the affected side is excessively elevated to compensate for knee stiffness."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FOOT DRAGGING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An abnormal gait pattern where there is decreased dorsiflexion due to knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REDUCED STEP LENGTH&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An abnormal gait pattern where the step length on the affected side is shorter due to knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INCREASED HIP EXTERNAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An abnormal gait pattern where the hip externally rotates to offload knee flexion stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Increased Hip External Rotation is a movement where the hip is rotated outward to offload knee flexion stress."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REDUCED KNEE FLEXION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An abnormal movement pattern during squatting and sit-to-stand movements, where the individual cannot reach normal depth due to knee pain."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reduced Knee Flexion is the inability to reach normal depth during squatting, often seen in knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FORWARD TRUNK LEAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An abnormal movement pattern during squatting and sit-to-stand movements, where the individual increases hip flexion to reduce knee demand."&lt;SEP&gt;"Forward Trunk Lean is an increased hip flexion to reduce knee demand during squatting."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VALGUS COLLAPSE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An abnormal movement pattern during squatting and sit-to-stand movements, where the knee collapses inward."&lt;SEP&gt;"Valgus Collapse is the medial displacement of the knee due to weak glutes or pain avoidance."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP IMPINGEMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where there is pain during hip flexion, often due to structural abnormalities in the hip joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP CONTRACTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the range of motion in the hip is limited, often due to scar tissue or muscle tightness."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP INSTABILITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the hip joint is unstable, often leading to asymmetry in abduction and adduction movements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP ARTHRITIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the hip joint is inflamed, leading to reduced range of motion in adduction movements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FAI SYNDROME&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Femoroacetabular impingement syndrome, a condition where there is limited internal rotation in the hip joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MENISCUS TEARS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the meniscus in the knee is torn, leading to limited internal rotation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IT BAND SYNDROME&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the iliotibial band is inflamed, leading to pain during external rotation of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ACHILLES TIGHTNESS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the Achilles tendon is tight, leading to limited dorsiflexion in the ankle."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POSTERIOR IMPINGEMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where there is pain during plantarflexion, often due to bony or soft tissue impingement in the ankle."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ANKLE SPRAINS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the ligaments in the ankle are sprained, leading to limited inversion movements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PERONEAL TENDON DYSFUNCTION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition where the peroneal tendons are dysfunctional, leading to reduced eversion movements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CYNTHIA C. NORKIN, PT, EDD&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cynthia C. Norkin is a physical therapist and educator, contributing to the chapter on the structure and function of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A body part consisting of the tibiofemoral and patellofemoral joints, with specific anatomical structures and functions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Knee is a part of the human body discussed in the context of its anatomy and range of motion, including flexion and extension."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Knee is a section of the text that discusses the structure and function of the knee joint, including its components and movements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"One of the two articulations of the knee, involving the femur and tibia, with specific anatomical features and movements."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Tibiofemoral Joint is one of the two main articulations of the knee, involving the femur and tibia bones."&lt;SEP&gt;"The tibiofemoral joint is a double condyloid joint with two degrees of freedom, allowing flexion-extension and rotation. It involves the tibia and femur and is stabilized by various ligaments and muscles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PATELLOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"One of the two articulations of the knee, involving the patella and femur, with specific anatomical features and movements."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Patellofemoral Joint is the second main articulation of the knee, involving the patella and femur bones."&lt;SEP&gt;"The patellofemoral joint is the articulation between the patella and the femur, involving the posterior surface of the patella and the femoral patellar surface. It is stabilized by the quadriceps tendon and the patellar ligament."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MENISCI&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Menisci are the joint discs located on the tibial condyles, providing cushioning and stability to the knee joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"Two C-shaped pieces of cartilage that sit on the tibial condyles, providing cushioning and stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERCONDYLAR NOTCH&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A deep groove on the femur that separates the medial and lateral condyles."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Intercondylar Notch is a deep groove between the medial and lateral condyles of the femur, visible on the posterior and inferior aspects of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERCONDYLAR TUBERCLES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Intercondylar Tubercles are bony spines that separate the medial and lateral condyles of the tibia, providing attachment points for ligaments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Two bony spines on the tibia that separate the medial and lateral condyles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FEMORAL PATELLAR SURFACE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Femoral Patellar Surface is the area on the anterior part of the femur where it articulates with the patella."&lt;SEP&gt;"The area on the femur where the patella articulates, located anteriorly between the condyles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAL CONDYLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Medial Condyle is the inner rounded projection at the end of the femur, forming part of the tibiofemoral joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larger, more prominent condyle of the femur, located on the inner side of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL CONDYLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Lateral Condyle is the outer rounded projection at the end of the femur, forming part of the tibiofemoral joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"The smaller condyle of the femur, located on the outer side of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TIBIA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Tibia is the larger of the two lower leg bones, forming the lower part of the tibiofemoral joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"The tibia is a bone in the lower leg, featuring lateral and medial condyles and intercondylar tubercles. It is part of the tibiofemoral joint and interacts with the femur and patella."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FEMUR&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Femur is the longest and strongest bone in the human body, located in the thigh."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Femur is the thigh bone, forming the upper part of the tibiofemoral joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"The femur is the thigh bone, featuring medial and lateral condyles that articulate with the tibia. It is part of the tibiofemoral joint and interacts with the patella."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PATELLA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Patella, also known as the kneecap, is a triangular bone that articulates with the femur at the patellofemoral joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"The kneecap, a triangular bone located in front of the knee joint, to which the quadriceps tendon attaches and continues as the patella tendon."&lt;SEP&gt;"The patella, also known as the kneecap, is a triangular bone that articulates with the femur and is part of the patellofemoral joint. It is connected to the quadriceps tendon and the patellar ligament."&lt;SEP&gt;"The patella, or kneecap, is a small bone located in front of the knee joint, which slides in a groove on the end of the femur."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Pain is a condition that affects the knee, leading to various compensatory movement patterns and gait deviations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CIRCUMDUCTION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Circumduction is an outward swinging of the leg to avoid bending the knee, a compensatory movement pattern seen in knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DECREASED STEP LENGTH&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Decreased Step Length is a shorter step taken on the affected side to reduce knee stress."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SQUAT &amp; SIT-TO-STAND MOVEMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Squat &amp; Sit-to-Stand Movements are activities that involve bending and standing, often affected by knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEEL ELEVATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Heel Elevation is lifting the heels to avoid loading the quadriceps, a common compensatory movement in knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;UNEVEN WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Uneven Weight Distribution is placing more weight on the non-affected leg to avoid loading the painful knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STAIRS (ASCENDING &amp; DESCENDING)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Stairs (Ascending &amp; Descending) are activities that involve going up and down stairs, often affected by knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LEADING WITH NON-AFFECTED LEG&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Leading with Non-Affected Leg is a strategy to avoid loading the painful knee during stair climbing."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RANGE OF MOTION (ROM) DEFICIENCIES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Range of Motion (ROM) Deficiencies are limitations in the movement of the knee joint, often seen in knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PAIN AT END-RANGE MOTION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Pain at End-Range Motion is limited movement due to discomfort, often experienced at the extremes of knee motion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;JOINT LOADING &amp; ASYMMETRY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Joint Loading &amp; Asymmetry refers to the uneven distribution of forces across joints, often seen in knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INCREASED HIP AND ANKLE LOAD&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Increased Hip and Ankle Load is a compensatory mechanism to avoid knee motion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DECREASED QUADRICEPS ACTIVATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Decreased Quadriceps Activation is reduced knee extension force, often seen in knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TENNIS ELBOW (LATERAL EPICONDYLITIS)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Tennis Elbow (Lateral Epicondylitis) is a condition affecting the elbow, leading to various compensatory movement patterns."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REDUCED WRIST EXTENSION STRENGTH&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Reduced Wrist Extension Strength is weakness in the wrist extensors, a common symptom of tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OVERUSE OF FINGER FLEXORS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Overuse of Finger Flexors is excessive grip strength instead of wrist stabilization, a compensatory movement in tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXCESSIVE WRIST FLEXION WITH GRIPPING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Excessive Wrist Flexion with Gripping is a movement to avoid engaging wrist extensors, seen in tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SHOULDER &amp; ELBOW MOVEMENT PATTERNS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Shoulder &amp; Elbow Movement Patterns are activities involving the shoulder and elbow, often affected by tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INCREASED SHOULDER ABDUCTION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Increased Shoulder Abduction is lifting the arm excessively to compensate for weak forearm muscles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REDUCED ELBOW EXTENSION DURING ACTIVITIES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Reduced Elbow Extension During Activities is avoiding full extension due to pain, a common compensatory movement in tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OVERUSE OF UPPER TRAPEZIUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Overuse of Upper Trapezius is shrugging the shoulder while gripping or lifting objects, a compensatory movement in tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ASYMMETRICAL FORCE APPLICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Asymmetrical Force Application is uneven distribution of grip strength, often seen in tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COMPENSATORY FINGER ACTIVATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Compensatory Finger Activation is using more finger flexors instead of wrist extensors, a common compensatory movement in tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXCESSIVE FOREARM SUPINATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Excessive Forearm Supination is a movement to avoid painful pronation, often seen in tennis elbow."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AVOIDANCE OF REPETITIVE WRIST EXTENSION MOVEMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Avoidance of Repetitive Wrist Extension Movements is a strategy to reduce pain, often seen in activities like typing, lifting, and gripping."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BACK PAIN (LUMBAR AND THORACIC)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Back Pain (Lumbar and Thoracic) is a condition affecting the back, leading to various compensatory movement patterns and gait deviations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GAIT &amp; POSTURAL DEVIATIONS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Gait &amp; Postural Deviations are changes in walking and posture, often seen in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INCREASED PELVIC TILT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Increased Pelvic Tilt is excessive anterior or posterior tilt to reduce pain, often seen in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REDUCED SPINAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Reduced Spinal Rotation is avoiding twisting due to pain, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LIFTING &amp; BENDING MOVEMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Lifting &amp; Bending Movements are activities involving lifting and bending, often affected by back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXCESSIVE KNEE BENDING INSTEAD OF HIP HINGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Excessive Knee Bending Instead of Hip Hinge is using the knees more than necessary to avoid lumbar stress, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUNDED BACK WHILE BENDING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Rounded Back While Bending is increased thoracic flexion due to lumbar instability, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ASYMMETRIC WEIGHT SHIFTING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Asymmetric Weight Shifting is leaning to one side to unload pain, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SITTING POSTURE &amp; ERGONOMICS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Sitting Posture &amp; Ergonomics are the postures and ergonomic practices while sitting, often affected by back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FORWARD HEAD POSTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Forward Head Posture is a common issue in chronic back pain sufferers, leading to poor posture."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INCREASED THORACIC KYPHOSIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Increased Thoracic Kyphosis is a rounded upper back due to compensatory movement, often seen in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXCESSIVE RELIANCE ON ARMRESTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Excessive Reliance on Armrests is reducing spinal engagement by supporting weight with arms, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CORE &amp; HIP STABILITY ANALYSIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Core &amp; Hip Stability Analysis involves assessing the stability of the core and hips, often affected by back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DECREASED GLUTE ACTIVATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Decreased Glute Activation is avoiding hip engagement in movements like bridges or squats, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXCESSIVE HIP FLEXOR ACTIVATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Excessive Hip Flexor Activation is overuse of iliopsoas to stabilize the spine, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INCREASED PARASPINAL MUSCLE STIFFNESS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Increased Paraspinal Muscle Stiffness is over-recruitment of spinal erectors, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BREATHING PATTERNS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Breathing Patterns involve the way a person breathes, often affected by back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SHALLOW CHEST BREATHING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Shallow Chest Breathing is a common issue in back pain, where the person uses the chest instead of the diaphragm."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OVERUSE OF NECK MUSCLES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Overuse of Neck Muscles is using SCM and scalene muscles for breathing instead of deep core engagement, a common compensatory movement in back pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COMPUTER VISION &amp; POSE ESTIMATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Computer Vision &amp; Pose Estimation are technologies used to detect deviations from ideal movement patterns, often used in tracking and detecting compensations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WEARABLE SENSORS (IMUS)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Wearable Sensors (IMUs) are devices that capture ROM and joint angles dynamically, often used in tracking and detecting compensations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PRESSURE SENSORS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Pressure Sensors are devices that analyze weight distribution in foot and hand pressure, often used in tracking and detecting compensations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELECTROMYOGRAPHY (EMG) SENSORS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Electromyography (EMG) Sensors are devices that measure muscle activation patterns for compensations, often used in tracking and detecting compensations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;QUADRICEPS TENDON&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A tendon that connects the quadriceps muscles to the patella, providing anterior stability to the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The quadriceps tendon is a strong tendon that connects the quadriceps muscle to the patella, providing anterior stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PATELLAR LIGAMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A ligament that connects the patella to the tibia, providing anterior stability to the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The patellar ligament is a continuation of the quadriceps tendon, connecting the patella to the tibial tuberosity, providing anterior stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL MENISCUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The lateral meniscus is a C-shaped fibrocartilage that sits on the lateral condyle of the tibia, providing cushioning and stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAL MENISCUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The medial meniscus is a C-shaped fibrocartilage that sits on the medial condyle of the tibia, providing cushioning and stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ANTERIOR CRUCIATE LIGAMENT (ACL)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) is a crucial stabilizer of the knee, preventing anterior displacement of the tibia relative to the femur. It is located within the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POSTERIOR CRUCIATE LIGAMENT (PCL)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) is a crucial stabilizer of the knee, preventing posterior displacement of the tibia relative to the femur. It is located within the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAL COLLATERAL LIGAMENT (MCL)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The medial collateral ligament (MCL) is a ligament on the inner side of the knee, providing medial stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL COLLATERAL LIGAMENT (LCL)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The lateral collateral ligament (LCL) is a ligament on the outer side of the knee, providing lateral stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ILIOTIBIAL BAND (ITB)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The iliotibial band (ITB) is a thick band of fibrous tissue running down the outside of the thigh, providing lateral stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PES ANSERINUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A group of tendons from the sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus muscles that attach to the tibia, providing medial-lateral stability to the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The pes anserinus is a group of tendons from the sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus muscles that attach to the tibia, providing medial stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SCREW-HOME MECHANISM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An automatic rotation that locks the knee in a fully extended position, occurring during the last 10 to 15 degrees of extension."&lt;SEP&gt;"The screw-home mechanism is an automatic rotation that occurs during the last 10 to 15 degrees of knee extension, where the tibia laterally rotates to lock the knee in the fully extended position."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CAPSULAR PATTERN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A capsular pattern is defined as a ratio of extension loss to flexion loss between 0.03 and 0.50, indicating a specific type of joint restriction."&lt;SEP&gt;"The capsular pattern at the knee is characterized by a smaller limitation of extension compared to flexion, with no restriction of rotations. It can be associated with conditions like arthritis."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SEMITENDINOSUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Semitendinosus is another muscle mentioned in the context of causing tension in the leg, contributing to the firmness of the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The semitendinosus is a muscle that contributes to the pes anserinus, providing medial stability to the knee joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"The semitendinosus is one of the hamstring muscles, originating from the ischial tuberosity and inserting on the proximal aspect of the medial surface of the tibia."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GRACILIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The gracilis is a muscle that contributes to the pes anserinus, providing medial stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SARTORIUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The sartorius is a muscle that contributes to the pes anserinus, providing medial stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TIBIAL TUBEROSITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A bony prominence on the tibia where the patella tendon attaches, facilitating the transmission of force from the quadriceps muscles to the tibia."&lt;SEP&gt;"The tibial tuberosity is a bony prominence on the tibia where the patellar ligament attaches, providing anterior stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NON-WEIGHT-BEARING ACTIVE MOTION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Non-weight-bearing active motion refers to movements of the knee joint without the weight of the body, involving the sliding of the tibial condyles on the femoral condyles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AUTOMATIC ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An involuntary rotation that occurs at the knee joint, particularly during the last portion of active extension."&lt;SEP&gt;"Automatic rotation is an involuntary movement of the tibia relative to the femur, occurring primarily toward the end of knee extension to lock the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VOLUNTARY ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A conscious movement of the knee joint, possible when the knee is flexed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Voluntary rotation is a controlled movement of the tibia relative to the femur, possible when the knee is flexed."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A type of movement in the knee joint, often assessed in relation to joint health and disease progression."&lt;SEP&gt;"Internal rotation is a type of voluntary rotation where the tibia rotates medially relative to the femur."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXTERNAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"External rotation is a type of voluntary rotation where the tibia rotates laterally relative to the femur."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ARTHRITIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Arthritis is a condition characterized by inflammation and degeneration of the knee joint, often associated with a capsular pattern."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ARTHROSES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Arthroses refer to degenerative changes in the knee joint, often associated with a capsular pattern."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FRITZ AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Fritz and Associates conducted a study on the capsular pattern of the knee, finding a correlation between the ratio of extension loss to flexion loss and the likelihood of having arthritis or arthroses of the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fritz and associates conducted a study on patients with a capsular pattern and their likelihood of having arthritis or arthroses of the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fritz and associates conducted research on the capsular pattern of the knee, finding that patients with a specific ratio of extension loss to flexion loss were more likely to have arthritis or arthroses."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ALMQUIST AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Almquist and colleagues conducted a study on voluntary knee rotation, estimating the distribution of internal and external rotation in healthy individuals."&lt;SEP&gt;"Almquist and colleagues examined the reliability of the Rottameter for measuring knee rotation with applied torque at different flexion angles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Almquist and colleagues measured knee rotation in healthy women and men, finding that women had a significantly larger knee rotation ROM than men at all tested degrees of knee flexion and applied torques."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07ca63fd10cfd76d8fe746402f8a6481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ARTHRITIS OR ARTHROSES OF THE KNEE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Arthritis or arthroses of the knee refers to conditions affecting the knee joint, characterized by inflammation, pain, and degeneration of joint tissues."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RANGE OF MOTION TESTING PROCEDURES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Range of motion testing procedures are methods used to assess the extent of movement in a joint, typically using a goniometer to measure angles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GONIOMETER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Goniometer is a device used to measure the range of motion of a joint, particularly useful in orthopedic assessments."&lt;SEP&gt;"A goniometer is a device used to measure joint angles, commonly used in clinical settings to assess knee ROM."&lt;SEP&gt;"A goniometer is a tool used to measure angles, particularly useful in assessing the range of motion in joints such as the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"A goniometer is a tool used to measure the range of motion (ROM) of a joint, such as the knee. It consists of a fulcrum, a proximal arm, and a distal arm."&lt;SEP&gt;"A goniometer is a tool used to measure the range of motion in a joint, often used in physical therapy and orthopedic assessments."&lt;SEP&gt;"A goniometer is a tool used to measure the range of motion of joints, specifically mentioned for measuring the knee's position."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-907485f01764ad42a85685e6e4a21163</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE FLEXION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A movement of the knee joint where the angle between the thigh and lower leg decreases, often measured in studies of joint function and disease."&lt;SEP&gt;"Knee flexion is the movement of bending the knee, occurring in the sagittal plane around a medial-lateral axis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Knee flexion is the movement that occurs when the knee bends, reducing the angle between the thigh and the calf. It is typically measured from the starting position of knee extension."&lt;SEP&gt;"The movement of bending the knee, measured in degrees, required for various activities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE EXTENSION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A movement of the knee joint where the angle between the thigh and lower leg increases, often measured in studies of joint function and disease."&lt;SEP&gt;"Knee Extension is the movement of the knee joint where the lower leg straightens out, moving away from the thigh."&lt;SEP&gt;"Knee Extension refers to the measurement of how much the knee can extend beyond 0 degrees, which is a normal finding in young children but not typically observed in adults."&lt;SEP&gt;"Knee extension is a movement that occurs in the sagittal plane around a medial-lateral axis, typically starting at 0 degrees for adults. Limitations or hyperextensions can indicate issues with the knee joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"Knee extension is the movement of straightening the knee, also occurring in the sagittal plane around a medial-lateral axis."&lt;SEP&gt;"The movement of straightening the knee, measured in degrees, required for various activities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SQUATTING POSITION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The squatting position is a posture where the knees and hips are bent, often used in functional movements and exercises."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SUPINE POSITION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Supine Position is a body position where the person lies flat on their back, often used in medical examinations and tests."&lt;SEP&gt;"The supine position is a lying position on the back, commonly used in medical and therapeutic settings for assessments and treatments."&lt;SEP&gt;"The supine position is a non-weight-bearing position where the individual lies flat on their back, often used in medical examinations and physical therapy."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 9.4&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 9.4 is a diagram showing surface anatomy landmarks for goniometer alignment on the right lower extremity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 9.6&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 9.6 illustrates the right lower extremity at the end of knee flexion ROM, showing the examiner's technique."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 9.7&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 9.7 shows the starting position for measuring knee flexion ROM, with the individual in a supine position."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 9.8&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 9.8 depicts the end of the knee flexion ROM, with the examiner maintaining knee flexion and aligning the goniometer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL FEMORAL EPICONDYLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The lateral femoral epicondyle is a bony landmark on the femur where the body of the goniometer should be placed."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GREATER TROCHANTER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Greater Trochanter is a bony landmark used for aligning the proximal arm of the goniometer with the femur."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Greater Trochanter is a large, bony prominence on the upper part of the femur, used as a reference point for goniometer alignment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The greater trochanter is a bony landmark on the femur used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer's proximal arm."&lt;SEP&gt;"The greater trochanter is a bony prominence on the upper part of the femur, used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."&lt;SEP&gt;"The greater trochanter is a bony prominence on the upper part of the femur, used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during the Ely test."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL MALLEOLUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Lateral Malleolus is a bony landmark used for aligning the distal arm of the goniometer with the fibula."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lateral Malleolus is the bony prominence on the outer side of the ankle, used as a reference point for goniometer alignment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The lateral malleolus is a bony landmark on the fibula used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer's distal arm."&lt;SEP&gt;"The lateral malleolus is a bony prominence on the outer side of the ankle, used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."&lt;SEP&gt;"The lateral malleolus is a bony prominence on the outer side of the ankle, used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during the Ely test."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VASTUS MEDIALIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The vastus medialis is a muscle of the quadriceps femoris that can contribute to the firm end-feel during knee flexion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The vastus medialis is one of the four muscles of the quadriceps femoris, responsible for knee extension and crossing only the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VASTUS LATERALIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The vastus lateralis is a muscle of the quadriceps femoris that can contribute to the firm end-feel during knee flexion."&lt;SEP&gt;"The vastus lateralis is one of the four muscles of the quadriceps femoris, responsible for knee extension and crossing only the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VASTUS INTERMEDIALIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The vastus intermedialis is a muscle of the quadriceps femoris that can contribute to the firm end-feel during knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POSTERIOR CALF MUSCLES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The posterior calf muscles can contribute to the soft end-feel during knee flexion due to contact with the thigh or heel."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEAL AND BUTTOCKS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The heel and buttocks can contribute to the soft end-feel during knee flexion due to contact with the posterior calf muscles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NORMAL RANGE OF FLEXION FOR ADULTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The normal range of flexion for adults is typically between 130 to 140 degrees, though definitions vary."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AMA’S GUIDES TO THE EVALUATION OF PERMANENT IMPAIRMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The AMA’s Guides to the Evaluation of Permanent Impairment provide standards for assessing impairment, including a broader definition of normal knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TABLES 9.1 THROUGH 9.3&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Tables 9.1 through 9.3 contain additional normal ROM values by age and gender, providing detailed reference data for knee flexion and extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SAGITTAL PLANE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A plane that divides the body into left and right sections, where flexion-extension movements occur."&lt;SEP&gt;"The sagittal plane is a vertical plane that divides the body into left and right sections, where knee flexion and extension occur."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAL-LATERAL AXIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An axis running from the inner to the outer side of the knee, around which flexion-extension movements occur."&lt;SEP&gt;"The medial-lateral axis is the axis around which knee flexion and extension movements occur in the sagittal plane."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HYPEREXTENSION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Hyperextension of the knee occurs when the knee extends beyond the normal 0-degree starting position, potentially leading to conditions like genu recurvatum."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hyperextension refers to knee extension beyond 0 degrees, which is considered normal in young children but not in adults."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OBLIQUE AND ARCUATE POPLITEAL LIGAMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"These ligaments are part of the knee joint structure, contributing to stability and providing a firm end-feel during knee extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COLLATERAL LIGAMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The collateral ligaments are located on the sides of the knee and provide stability, contributing to the firm end-feel during knee extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ANTERIOR AND POSTERIOR CRUCIATE LIGAMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The cruciate ligaments are located inside the knee joint and provide stability, contributing to the firm end-feel during knee extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL EPICONDYLE OF THE FEMUR&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Lateral Epicondyle of the Femur is a bony landmark used for aligning the goniometer to measure knee position."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lateral Epicondyle of the Femur is a bony prominence on the outer side of the knee, used as a reference point for goniometer alignment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The lateral epicondyle of the femur is a bony landmark used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during the Ely test."&lt;SEP&gt;"The lateral epicondyle of the femur is a bony landmark used as the center fulcrum for aligning a goniometer during knee measurements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIBULAR HEAD&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Fibular Head is a bony landmark used for aligning the distal arm of the goniometer with the fibula."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Fibular Head is the upper end of the fibula bone, located just below the knee, used as a reference point for goniometer alignment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The fibular head is a bony prominence at the top of the fibula, used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."&lt;SEP&gt;"The fibular head is a bony prominence at the top of the fibula, used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during the Ely test."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee rotation involves the rotational movement of the tibia relative to the femur. Measuring this in a clinical setting is challenging and often not recommended due to ongoing research."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;QUADRICEPS FEMORIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The quadriceps femoris is a group of four muscles (rectus femoris, vastus medialis, vastus lateralis, and vastus intermedius) that are responsible for knee extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RECTUS FEMORIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The rectus femoris is one of the four muscles of the quadriceps femoris, unique in that it crosses both the hip and knee joints, allowing it to flex the hip and extend the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VASTUS INTERMEDIUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The vastus intermedius is one of the four muscles of the quadriceps femoris, responsible for knee extension and crossing only the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PATELLA TENDON&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The patella tendon, also known as the patellar ligament, is the continuation of the quadriceps tendon distal to the patella, attaching to the tibial tuberosity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELY TEST&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A specific test used to measure knee flexion ROM, often involving the use of an inclinometer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ely test is a specific test used to measure knee flexion range of motion (ROM)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ely test is a clinical test used to evaluate the length of the rectus femoris muscle by positioning the hip in 0 degrees of flexion and then flexing the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Ely test is a procedure used to assess the length of the rectus femoris muscle by positioning the hip in 0 degrees of flexion and then flexing the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-907485f01764ad42a85685e6e4a21163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PRONE POSITION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The prone position is a body position where the individual lies flat on their stomach, often used in medical examinations and physical therapy."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE FLEXION ROM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Flexion ROM (Range of Motion) is the measurement of how much the knee can bend from the starting position of knee extension."&lt;SEP&gt;"Knee Flexion ROM refers to the range of motion of the knee joint during flexion, which is influenced by factors such as age, gender, and body mass."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GENU RECURVATUM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Genu Recurvatum is a condition where the knee hyperextends, causing the leg to bend backward excessively."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RESEARCH FINDINGS AND TABLES 9.1 TO 9.4&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Research findings and tables that provide detailed information on knee extension ROM values for different age groups from neonates to older adults."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FOLDED TOWEL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A folded towel placed under the ankle to ensure the knee is in full extension during the measurement of knee flexion ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VOLUNTARY KNEE ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Voluntary knee rotation is the rotational movement of the tibia relative to the femur, which is currently not recommended for measurement in the clinical setting due to ongoing research."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OB MYRIN COMPASS INCLINOMETER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A tool used to measure total active tibial rotation ROM, developed by Clarkson and used in a sitting position with the knee flexed to 90 degrees."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TOTAL ACTIVE TIBIAL ROTATION ROM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The total active tibial rotation range of motion, measured in women and men, with mean values of 40 degrees and 58 degrees, respectively."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RELIABILITY AND VALIDITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The reliability and validity of a testing method refer to the consistency and accuracy of the measurements obtained."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ONE-JOINT MUSCLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A muscle that crosses only one joint, such as the vastus medialis, vastus lateralis, and vastus intermedius, which are responsible for knee extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TWO-JOINT MUSCLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A muscle that crosses more than one joint, such as the rectus femoris, which crosses both the hip and knee joints."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ISOLATED ROM TESTING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Isolated ROM testing involves evaluating the range of motion of a single joint to determine if the limitation is caused by joint structures or muscles crossing only one joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ANTERIOR INFERIOR ILIAC SPINE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A bony landmark on the pelvis from which the rectus femoris muscle originates, specifically the anterior tendon."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ACETABULUM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The socket in the pelvis where the head of the femur fits, providing a reference point for the origin of the rectus femoris muscle's posterior tendon."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELY TEST POSITION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The position used for the Ely test, where the individual is placed prone with both feet off the end of the examining table, hips in 0 degrees of flexion, and knees extended."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RECTUS FEMORIS MUSCLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The rectus femoris muscle is one of the quadriceps muscles located in the front of the thigh, responsible for extending the knee and flexing the hip."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GNAT AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Gnat and colleagues conducted a study involving 14 healthy university students to evaluate the range of motion during the Ely test."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gnat and colleagues conducted a study using a pleurimeter and force gauge to measure knee ROM."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gnat and colleagues evaluated the reliability of the passive knee extension test for hamstring muscle length, reporting excellent reliability."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-907485f01764ad42a85685e6e4a21163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;UNIVERSITY STUDENT GROUP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A group of 30 healthy university students from which 14 were selected for a study on the Ely test."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HAMSTRING MUSCLES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Hamstring Muscles are a group of muscles that include the semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and biceps femoris, and are important for knee function."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hamstring muscles are a group of three muscles located in the back of the thigh, responsible for extending the hip and flexing the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SEMIMEMBRANOSUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Semimembranosus is one of the muscles mentioned in the context of causing tension in the leg, contributing to the firmness of the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The semimembranosus is one of the hamstring muscles, originating from the ischial tuberosity and inserting on the medial aspect of the medial tibial condyle."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BICEPS FEMORIS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Biceps Femoris is a muscle mentioned in the context of causing tension in the leg, contributing to the firmness of the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biceps femoris is a two-headed muscle of the hamstring group, with origins from the ischial tuberosity and the sacrotuberous ligament, and insertions on the head of the fibula and the lateral tibial condyle."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL MIDLINE OF THE FEMUR&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Lateral Midline of the Femur is a reference line used for aligning the proximal arm of the goniometer."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Lateral Midline of the Femur is the imaginary line running down the center of the outer side of the thigh, used for goniometer alignment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The lateral midline of the femur is a reference line used for aligning the goniometer during the Ely test."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ISCHIAL TUBEROSITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The ischial tuberosity is a bony prominence on the pelvis, serving as the origin for several muscles, including the semitendinosus and semimembranosus."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAL SURFACE OF THE TIBIA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The medial surface of the tibia is a bony area on the inner side of the shinbone, where the semitendinosus muscle inserts."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAL TIBIAL CONDYLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The medial tibial condyle is a bony prominence on the inner side of the knee, where the semimembranosus muscle inserts."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL LIP OF THE LINEA ASPERA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The lateral lip of the linea aspera is a bony ridge on the femur, where the short head of the biceps femoris muscle originates."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL SUPRACONDYLAR LINE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The lateral supracondylar line is a bony ridge on the femur, where the short head of the biceps femoris muscle originates."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL INTERMUSCULAR SEPTUM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The lateral intermuscular septum is a connective tissue structure that separates the muscles of the thigh, where the short head of the biceps femoris muscle originates."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEAD OF THE FIBULA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The head of the fibula is a bony prominence at the top of the fibula, where the biceps femoris muscle inserts."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL TIBIAL CONDYLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The lateral tibial condyle is a bony prominence on the outer side of the knee, where the biceps femoris muscle inserts."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL COLLATERAL LIGAMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A ligament that provides medial-lateral stability to the knee, located on the outer side of the joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"The lateral collateral ligament is a stabilizing ligament on the outer side of the knee, where the biceps femoris muscle inserts."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POPLITEAL ANGLE (PA) TEST&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Popliteal Angle (PA) Test is another name for the Distal Hamstring Length Test, which measures the angle between the femur and the lower leg to assess hamstring flexibility."&lt;SEP&gt;"The popliteal angle (PA) test is a procedure used to assess the length of the hamstring muscles by measuring the angle between the femur and the lower leg."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DISTAL HAMSTRING LENGTH TEST&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Distal Hamstring Length Test, also known as the Hamstring Passive Knee Extension (PKE) Test or Popliteal Angle (PA) Test, measures the flexibility of the hamstring muscles by assessing the popliteal angle between the femur and the lower leg."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HAMSTRING PASSIVE KNEE EXTENSION (PKE) TEST&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Hamstring Passive Knee Extension (PKE) Test is another name for the Distal Hamstring Length Test, which measures the flexibility of the hamstring muscles by extending the knee and observing the resistance point."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GAJDOSIK AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Gajdosik and Associates conducted a study on the length of the hamstring muscles in healthy males, finding a mean value of 31 degrees with a standard deviation of 7.5 degrees."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gajdosik and associates studied the intrarater reliability of the knee extension test, finding high reliability for both active and passive measurements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;YOUDAS AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Youdas and Colleagues used a 360-degree universal goniometer to measure the popliteal angle in 214 individuals, reporting mean values of 152.0 degrees for women and 141.4 degrees for men."&lt;SEP&gt;"Youdas and colleagues examined the effects of age and gender on hamstring muscle length, finding significant differences between genders."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GNAT AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Gnat and Associates investigated the interrater and test-retest reliability of a modified version of the PKE test, using a belt to secure the pelvis and a goniometer fastened to the lower leg."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FREDRIKSEN AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Fredriksen and Colleagues found that PKE angle measurements for a single female subject tested 16 times per side ranged from 153 to 159 degrees for the left leg and from 154 to 165 degrees for the right leg."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE EXTENSION LIMITATIONS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Extension Limitations refer to the normal limitations in knee extension observed in newborns, which typically resolve by around 12 to 13 years of age."&lt;SEP&gt;"Knee Extension Limitations refer to the reduced range of motion in the knee joint, particularly in newborns and young children, which typically resolves by adolescence."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NEONATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Neonates are infants in the first 3 days of life, studied for their knee extension limitations, which are similar to those of newborns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Neonates are newborn infants, typically within the first month of life, who often exhibit knee extension limitations of 15 to 20 degrees at birth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Neonates are newborns, specifically within the first 28 days of life, often studied for their physical development, including knee extension limitations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP FLEXION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Hip Flexion is the movement of the hip joint where the thigh moves forward and upward, bringing the knee closer to the chest."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LOWER LEG&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Lower Leg is the part of the leg between the knee and the ankle, consisting of the tibia and fibula bones."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POPLITEAL ANGLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Popliteal Angle is the angle formed between the femur and the lower leg, measured during the Distal Hamstring Length Test."&lt;SEP&gt;"The angle formed at the back of the knee, used as a measurement in physical therapy and orthopedic assessments."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7abf467a8b1da896b788e651526495</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SEMITENDINOSUS MUSCLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Semitendinosus Muscle is one of the hamstring muscles located in the posterior thigh, contributing to knee flexion and hip extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SEMIMEMBRANOSUS MUSCLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Semimembranosus Muscle is one of the hamstring muscles located in the posterior thigh, contributing to knee flexion and hip extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BICEPS FEMORIS MUSCLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Biceps Femoris Muscle is one of the hamstring muscles located in the posterior thigh, contributing to knee flexion and hip extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STANDARD DEVIATION (SD)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Standard Deviation (SD) is a statistical measure that quantifies the amount of variation or dispersion in a set of values."&lt;SEP&gt;"Standard Deviation (SD) is a statistical measure used in the research findings to indicate the variability of the data."&lt;SEP&gt;"Standard deviation (SD) is a statistical measure used to describe the variability or dispersion of a dataset, often used in research studies."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;UNIVERSAL GONIOMETER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Universal Goniometer is a tool used to measure joint angles, commonly used in physical therapy and orthopedic assessments."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Universal Goniometer is a type of goniometer that can measure a wide range of joint movements, including those of the knee and hip."&lt;SEP&gt;"A tool used for measuring angles, particularly in medical settings, such as knee range of motion (ROM) measurements."&lt;SEP&gt;"A tool used to measure the range of motion in the knee joint, showing higher intratester reliability compared to intertester reliability."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7abf467a8b1da896b788e651526495&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FORCE GAUGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Force Gauge is a device used to measure the force applied during a test, often used in conjunction with a goniometer."&lt;SEP&gt;"A force gauge is a tool used to measure the amount of force required to produce passive extension or flexion of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-907485f01764ad42a85685e6e4a21163</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DYNAMOMETER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Dynamometer is a device used to measure force, torque, or power, often used in biomechanical assessments."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PELVIC BELT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Pelvic Belt is a device used to stabilize the pelvis during certain medical tests, ensuring consistent measurements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;APEX OF THE PATELLA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Apex of the Patella is the lower tip of the kneecap, used as a reference point for goniometer alignment."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINE JOINING THE TWO MALLEOLI&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Line Joining the Two Malleoli is an imaginary line connecting the inner and outer ankle bones, used as a reference point for goniometer alignment."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXTENSION LIMITATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Extension Limitation is a condition where the range of motion in a joint, such as the knee, is reduced, often seen in neonates."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FLEXION CONTRACTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A flexion contracture is an abnormal condition caused by fixed muscle shortness, which may be permanent and is distinct from extension limitations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Flexion Contracture is an abnormal condition where a joint, such as the knee, remains in a flexed position due to fixed muscle shortness."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NEONATAL DEVELOPMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Neonatal Development refers to the growth and changes that occur in newborn infants, including the gradual resolution of knee extension limitations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NEWBORNS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Newborns are infants in the first few weeks of life, characterized by a lack of full knee extension, which improves with age."&lt;SEP&gt;"Newborns are the subjects of several studies mentioned, focusing on their limited knee extension capabilities at birth and how these limitations change over time."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WAUGH AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Waugh and colleagues conducted research on knee extension limitations in newborns, finding a lack of approximately 15 to 20 degrees of knee extension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DREWS AND COWORKERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Drews and coworkers also studied knee extension limitations in newborns, reporting similar findings to Waugh and colleagues."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SCHWARZE AND DENTON&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Schwarze and Denton observed no gender differences in knee ROM in neonates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Schwarze and Denton observed no gender differences in knee ROM in newborns, suggesting that gender differences may not be evident at this early stage of development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Schwarze and Denton studied 1,000 neonates and found a mean extension limitation of 15 degrees in the first 3 days of life."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WANATABE AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Wanatabe and Associates conducted a study on knee extension in newborns and 2-year-olds, finding that newborns lack about 14 degrees of knee extension, while 2-year-olds have no such limitation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wanatabe and associates found that newborns lacked 14 degrees of knee extension, and by 2 years of age, this limitation was resolved, with some children exhibiting hyperextension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BROUGHTON, WRIGHT, AND MENELAUS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Broughton, Wright, and Menelaus measured extension limitations in neonates at birth and at 3 and 6 months, observing a significant reduction over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Broughton, Wright, and Menelaus measured extension limitations in normal neonates at birth, 3 months, and 6 months, observing a reduction in these limitations over time."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BOONE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Boone noted a mean of 5.4 degrees of extension beyond 0 degrees in children between 1 and 5 years of age."&lt;SEP&gt;"Boone noted that children between 1 year and 5 years of age had a mean of 5.4 degrees of extension beyond 0 degrees."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BEIGHTON, SOLOMON, AND SOSKOLNE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Beighton, Solomon, and Soskolne defined hyperextension of the knee and included it in a study of joint laxity, finding that females had more joint laxity than males."&lt;SEP&gt;"Beighton, Solomon, and Soskolne defined hyperextension of the knee as more than 10 degrees beyond 0 and found that females had more joint laxity than males at any age."&lt;SEP&gt;"Beighton, Solomon, and Soskolne studied joint laxity in 1,081 individuals, finding that joint laxity decreases rapidly throughout childhood and more slowly in adulthood."&lt;SEP&gt;"Beighton, Solomon, and Soskolne studied joint laxity in 1081 males and females, finding that joint laxity decreases rapidly throughout childhood and more slowly during adulthood."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MOLLINGER AND STEFFAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A research group that collected intratester reliability data on measurement of knee extension using a universal goniometer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mollinger and Steffan assessed knee ROM in nursing home residents, finding that only 13% had full passive knee extension and 42% had greater than 10 degrees of extension limitations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mollinger and Steffan collected intratester reliability data on knee extension measurements using the UG, finding high ICCs with small differences between measurements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STEULTJENS AND COWORKERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Steultjens and coworkers found knee flexion contractures in 31.5% of 198 patients with osteoarthritis of the knee or hip, noting that these contractures were associated with increased disability."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ERSOZ AND ERGUN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Ersoz and Ergun are researchers who studied the relationship between knee ROM measurements and radiographic findings in patients with bilateral knee osteoarthritis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ersoz and Ergun found that 82.5 percent of 44- to 76-year-old patients with primary knee osteoarthritis had extension limitations ranging from 1 to 14 degrees."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ersoz and Ergun studied 44- to 76-year-old patients with primary knee osteoarthritis, finding that 82.5% of the knees tested had extension limitations ranging from 1 to 14 degrees."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who studied the relationship between knee range of motion (ROM) measurements and radiographic findings in patients with bilateral knee osteoarthritis."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ESCALANTE AND COWORKERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Escalante and coworkers conducted a study on the relationship between obesity and passive knee flexion ROM."&lt;SEP&gt;"Escalante and coworkers found that female subjects had slightly reduced passive knee flexion ROM compared to males of the same age, possibly due to higher BMI in women."&lt;SEP&gt;"Escalante and coworkers found that female subjects had slightly reduced passive knee flexion ROM compared to males, possibly due to higher BMI."&lt;SEP&gt;"Escalante and coworkers measured knee flexion passive ROM in 687 community-dwelling elderly subjects, finding that 90.1 percent had more than 90 degrees of knee flexion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Escalante and coworkers measured knee flexion passive ROM in 687 community-dwelling elderly subjects, finding that 90.1% had more than 90 degrees of knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NONAKA AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Nonaka and colleagues conducted a study on age-related changes in hip and knee passive ROM in male volunteers, finding that hip joint ROM decreases with age while knee joint ROM remains unchanged."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nonaka and colleagues conducted a study on age-related changes in the hip and knee joints, finding that hip joint PROM decreases with age while knee joint PROM remains unchanged."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nonaka and colleagues examined age-related changes at the hip and knee, contributing to the understanding of joint mobility in different age groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nonaka and colleagues examined age-related changes in the hip and knee in 77 healthy male volunteers aged 15 to 73 years, focusing on passive range of motion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP JOINT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The hip joint is a part of the body that can experience extension limitations, similar to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ADULTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A demographic group characterized by a standard range of knee extension, typically 0 degrees."&lt;SEP&gt;"Adults are individuals who may have slightly less than full knee extension compared to young children."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COMMUNITY-DWELLING ELDERLY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Community-dwelling elderly are older adults living in the community, as opposed to institutional settings, and are often studied for their physical capabilities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OSTEOARTHRITIS PATIENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Osteoarthritis patients are individuals suffering from osteoarthritis, a degenerative joint disease that can affect the knee and hip."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NURSING HOME RESIDENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Nursing home residents are older adults living in institutional settings, often studied for their physical health and mobility."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PASSIVE RANGE OF MOTION (ROM)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Passive Range of Motion (ROM) is the range of movement of a joint when the muscles are relaxed and the movement is performed by an external force."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;UNIVERSAL GONIOMETER (UG)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Universal Goniometer (UG) is a tool used to measure joint angles and ranges of motion in clinical and research settings."&lt;SEP&gt;"A tool used for measuring joint angles, specifically in the context of knee flexion, with high intertester reliability and validity."&lt;SEP&gt;"A tool used to measure the range of motion (ROM) in joints, often with 25-centimeter arms and 1-degree increments."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Universal Goniometer (UG) is a tool used to measure joint angles, particularly the range of motion (ROM) of the knee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Universal Goniometer (UG) is a traditional tool used to measure knee flexion, used as a reference in the study."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8823385d2274c1cb6b908f4fb041f76c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AMERICAN ACADEMY OF ORTHOPAEDIC SURGEONS (AAOS)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons (AAOS) is a professional organization that sets standards and guidelines for orthopedic care."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DISABILITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Disability refers to the state of being unable to perform certain activities due to physical or mental impairments."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MACEDO AND MAGEE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Macedo and Magee conducted a study on goniometric passive ROM measurements in various joints of healthy Caucasian women, finding that increasing age is associated with small decreases in knee flexion ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SOUCIE AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Soucie and colleagues found that females generally have greater joint mobility than males across all age groups and nearly all joints."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LOUDON, GOIST, AND LOUDON&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Loudon, Goist, and Loudon defined knee hyperextension and observed that it is more common in females, potentially leading to functional deficits and increased risk of ACL injury."&lt;SEP&gt;"Loudon, Goist, and Loudon defined knee hyperextension as more than 5 degrees beyond the 0 position and found that hyperextension is more common in females and may be associated with functional deficits."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HALL AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Hall and colleagues found that female patients with hypermobility syndrome had alterations in proprioceptive acuity at the knee compared to a control group."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hall and colleagues found that female patients with hypermobility syndrome had altered proprioceptive acuity at the knee compared to a control group."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;JAMES AND PARKER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"James and Parker studied knee flexion ROM in elderly men and women, finding that women had greater ROM in both active and passive knee flexion than men."&lt;SEP&gt;"James and Parker studied knee flexion ROM in elderly men and women, finding that women had greater ROM than men, though overall values were lower than expected."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CLEFFKEN AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cleffken and colleagues found no gender differences in active and passive knee flexion and extension ROM in young adult volunteers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cleffken and colleagues found no gender differences in knee flexion and extension ROM in young adults."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WORLD HEALTH ORGANIZATION (WHO)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The WHO is an international organization that defines body mass index (BMI) and classifies overweight and obesity based on BMI values."&lt;SEP&gt;"The World Health Organization (WHO) is an international organization responsible for public health and provides standards and guidelines, including those for BMI classification."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LICHTENSTEIN AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Lichtenstein and associates conducted a study on the relationship between BMI and knee range of motion in elderly subjects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lichtenstein and associates found that elderly subjects with high BMI had lower knee ROM compared to those with low BMI."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lichtenstein and associates found that high BMI in elderly subjects was associated with lower knee ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;77 HEALTHY MALE VOLUNTEERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"77 healthy male volunteers aged 15 to 73 years were studied by Nonaka and colleagues to examine age-related changes in hip and knee joints."&lt;SEP&gt;"A group of 77 healthy male volunteers aged 15 to 73 years, studied by Nonaka and colleagues for age-related changes in hip and knee passive ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;90 HEALTHY CAUCASIAN WOMEN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A group of 90 healthy Caucasian women aged 18 to 59, studied by Macedo and Magee for goniometric passive ROM measurements in various joints."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1,081 MALES AND FEMALES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A large group of 1,081 males and females studied by Beighton, Solomon, and Soskolne for joint laxity, including knee hyperextension."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;60 HEALTHY WOMEN AND 60 HEALTHY MALE VOLUNTEERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A group of 60 healthy women and 60 healthy male volunteers aged 15 to older than 60, studied by Almquist and colleagues for knee rotation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;80 MEN AND WOMEN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"80 men and women aged 70 to older than 85 years were studied by James and Parker to examine knee flexion ROM."&lt;SEP&gt;"A group of 80 men and women aged 70 to older than 85, studied by James and Parker for knee flexion ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;527 GIRLS AND 473 BOYS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"527 girls and 473 boys aged 1 to 3 days were studied by Schwarze and Denton to observe gender differences in knee ROM."&lt;SEP&gt;"A group of 527 girls and 473 boys aged 1 to 3 days, studied by Schwarze and Denton for gender differences in knee ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;23 MALE AND 19 FEMALE HEALTHY VOLUNTEERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"23 male and 19 female healthy volunteers aged 19 to 27 years were studied by Cleffken and colleagues to examine gender differences in knee ROM."&lt;SEP&gt;"A group of 23 male and 19 female healthy volunteers aged 19 to 27, studied by Cleffken and colleagues for active and passive knee flexion and extension ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;647 COMMUNITY-DWELLING ELDERLY SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"647 community-dwelling elderly subjects aged 64 to 78 years were studied by Lichtenstein and associates to examine the impact of BMI on knee ROM."&lt;SEP&gt;"A group of 647 community-dwelling elderly subjects aged 64 to 78, studied by Lichtenstein and associates for the relationship between BMI and knee ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HYPEREXTENSION (GENURECURVATUM)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONDITION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition defined as more than 5 degrees of extension beyond the 0 position, associated with potential functional deficits in females."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HYPERMOBILITY SYNDROME&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONDITION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A condition characterized by alterations in proprioceptive acuity at the knee, studied in female patients by Hall and colleagues."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hypermobility syndrome is a condition characterized by joints that move beyond the normal range, which can affect proprioception and increase the risk of joint issues."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56ba6457f7a7f43d803a693f91176eca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BODY MASS INDEX (BMI)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A simple index of weight-for-height used to classify overweight and obesity, defined as a person’s weight in kilograms divided by the square of height in meters (kg/m2)."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OVERWEIGHT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONDITION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A classification for individuals with a BMI greater than or equal to 25, according to the WHO."&lt;SEP&gt;"Overweight is a category defined by the WHO as having a BMI greater than or equal to 25 but less than 30."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OBESE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONDITION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A classification for individuals with a BMI greater than or equal to 30, according to the WHO."&lt;SEP&gt;"Obese is a category defined by the WHO as having a BMI greater than or equal to 30."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4931980eb29da24ffb35c74359866d0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SOBTI AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Sobti and colleagues conducted a study on the association between obesity and the risk of pain or stiffness at the knee or hip in post office pensioners."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sobti and colleagues found that obesity was significantly associated with the risk of knee pain or stiffness in a survey of post office pensioners."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PARK AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Park and associates conducted a study comparing ROM measurements between obese and nonobese males."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MCCARTHY AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"McCarthy and colleagues used a sensor system to analyze gait in individuals with knee osteoarthritis (OA) and healthy controls."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;JEVSEVAR AND COWORKERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Jevsevar and coworkers measured knee ROM values required for various functional activities including gait and going up and down stairs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Jevsevar and coworkers measured the knee ROM required for various functional activities, finding that stair ascent required the greatest range of motion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OBERG, KARSZNIA, AND OBERG&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Oberg, Karsznia, and Oberg conducted a study on knee joint motion in midstance and swing phases of gait across a wide age range."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oberg, Karsznia, and Oberg used electrogoniometers to measure knee joint motion in the midstance and swing phases of gait across different age groups."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d1c052988db1f3bec75acfc56a5c3d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROWE AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Researchers who suggested a clinical guideline of 110 degrees of knee flexion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rowe and associates studied the amount of knee flexion required for various activities in elderly subjects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rowe and associates used a flexible electrogoniometer to measure knee joint motion in various activities, including walking, using stairs, and getting in and out of a chair and a bath."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d1c052988db1f3bec75acfc56a5c3d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LIVINGSTON, STEVENSON, AND OLNEY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Livingston, Stevenson, and Olney used testing staircases to measure knee flexion range during stair ascent and descent."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LAUBENTHAL, SMIDT, AND KETTLEKAMP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Laubenthal, Smidt, and Kettlekamp used an electrogoniometric method to measure knee motion in three planes during functional activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laubenthal, Smidt, and Kettlekamp used an electrogoniometric method to measure knee motion in three planes during stair use."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DESLOOVERE AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Desloovere and colleagues conducted a study measuring knee joint motion in volunteers during various motor tasks using gait-analysis techniques."&lt;SEP&gt;"Desloovere and colleagues conducted a study to analyze a set of motor tasks involving knee motion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Desloovere and colleagues measured knee joint motion in three anatomical planes during various motor tasks, finding significant differences in knee joint rotations during specific activities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8823385d2274c1cb6b908f4fb041f76c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07ca63fd10cfd76d8fe746402f8a6481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE OSTEOARTHRITIS (OA)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Osteoarthritis (OA) is a condition characterized by the deterioration of the cartilage in the knee joint, often associated with pain and reduced mobility."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RANGE OF MOTION (ROM)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Range of Motion (ROM) refers to the extent of movement possible at a joint, often measured using goniometers and radiographs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Range of Motion (ROM) refers to the extent of movement possible at a joint, which can be affected by factors such as obesity and age."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-704af454e8435279b724980dccc9f577&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GAIT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Gait refers to the manner or style of walking, which can be analyzed to understand biomechanical issues such as those related to knee osteoarthritis."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FUNCTIONAL ACTIVITIES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Functional Activities include daily tasks such as walking, going up and down stairs, and getting in and out of a chair, which require specific ranges of knee motion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional activities are daily tasks that require specific ranges of knee motion, such as stair ascent, gait, and rising from a chair."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COMMUNITY-DWELLING ELDERLY SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Community-Dwelling Elderly Subjects are older individuals living in the community, studied for their knee flexion range of motion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Community-dwelling elderly subjects are individuals aged 64 to 78 years who live in the community and were part of a study on BMI and knee ROM."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POST OFFICE PENSIONERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Post office pensioners are individuals who were part of a survey on the association between obesity and knee or hip pain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OBESE MALES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Obese males are a group of individuals with a BMI of 44, who were part of a study comparing ROM measurements with nonobese males."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NONOBESE MALES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Nonobese males are a group of individuals with a BMI of 22, who were part of a study comparing ROM measurements with obese males."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEALTHY INDIVIDUALS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Healthy individuals are individuals without knee osteoarthritis (OA) who were part of a study analyzing gait."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PATIENTS WITH KNEE OA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Patients with knee OA are individuals diagnosed with knee osteoarthritis who were part of a study analyzing gait."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELDERLY SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Elderly subjects are individuals aged 54 to 90 years who were part of a study on the amount of knee flexion required for various activities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEALTHY MALES AND FEMALES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Healthy males and females are individuals aged 10 to 79 years who were part of a study measuring knee joint motion during gait."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SHORTER SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Shorter subjects are individuals who had a greater maximum mean knee flexion range during stair ascent compared to taller subjects."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TALLER SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Taller subjects are individuals who had a lesser maximum mean knee flexion range during stair ascent compared to shorter subjects."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEALTHY YOUNG MALES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A category of subjects in a study with a mean age of 25 years, used to analyze knee motion for various activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Healthy young males are individuals with a mean age of 25 years who were part of a study measuring knee motion in three planes during stair use."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MOTOR TASKS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Motor tasks include a set of activities such as walking with sidestep and crossover turns, ascent onto and descent of a step, and mid- and deep squats."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d060789e6168c32660cca26addc6c1ce</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DESLOEREE AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A group of researchers who conducted a study analyzing a set of motor tasks including walking, sidestep and crossover turns, ascent and descent of a step, and others."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MCFAYDEN AND WINTER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"McFayden and Winter used specific staircase dimensions to measure knee motion during stair ascent and descent."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who studied the knee flexion ROM necessary for ascending and descending stairs using specific stair dimensions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PROTOPAPADAKI AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Protopapadaki and associates conducted a study on knee motion during stair ascent and descent using specific stair dimensions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protopapadaki and associates used specific staircase dimensions to measure knee motion during stair ascent and descent in young healthy adults."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who determined the knee motion during stair ascent and descent in young healthy male and female subjects."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-80cc1aeca75db3a2a6be0a84bff43fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1c052988db1f3bec75acfc56a5c3d59</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LARK AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Lark and colleagues compared knee ROM in stair descent between healthy elderly males and young males."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who compared knee ROM in stair descent between healthy elderly males and young males."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d1c052988db1f3bec75acfc56a5c3d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MULLHOLLAND AND WYSS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Authors who reviewed the literature on the functional range of knee motions required in non-Western cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mullholland and Wyss reviewed literature on the functional range of knee motions required in non-Western cultures for daily activities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d1c052988db1f3bec75acfc56a5c3d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEMMERICH AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Hemmerich and colleagues used an electrogoniometry motion tracking device to determine the range of motion needed for activities identified by Mullholland and Wyss."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who used an electrogoniometer to determine the range of motion needed for various activities in healthy Indian subjects."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d1c052988db1f3bec75acfc56a5c3d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SMITH AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Researchers who identified peak contact forces during squatting activities at high knee flexion angles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ZHOU AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A research group that used an Optotrack Certus 3020 tracking system to measure the ROM during kneeling postures in Chinese subjects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who used a three-dimensional tracking system to measure the ROM during a typical kneeling posture in healthy Chinese subjects."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEALTHY VOLUNTEERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A category of subjects in a study with a mean age of 29 years, used to analyze a set of motor tasks."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEALTHY INDIAN SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A category of subjects with an average age of 48 years, used to analyze the range of motion for various activities in non-Western cultures."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEALTHY CHINESE SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A category of subjects with an average age of 24 years, used to measure the ROM during a typical kneeling posture."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELDERLY MALES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A category of subjects with a mean age of 64 years, used to compare knee ROM in stair descent with younger males."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STAIR ASCENT AND DESCENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Activities involving moving up and down stairs, requiring specific ranges of knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SQUATTING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An activity that requires a large range of knee flexion, often exceeding 150 degrees."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CROSS-LEGGED SITTING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An activity common in non-Western cultures, requiring about 33 degrees of medial knee rotation and 37 degrees of lateral hip rotation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEELING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An activity that requires significant knee flexion, with variations based on the position of the ankles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHAIR RISE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An activity that involves rising from a seated position, requiring a mean range of knee flexion of 90 to 95 degrees."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PUTTING ON SOCKS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An activity that requires approximately 117 degrees of knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NON-WESTERN CULTURES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cultures where activities such as floor sitting, squatting, and kneeling are common, often requiring greater ranges of knee motion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SIDESTEP TURNS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An activity involving turning to the side, which requires a larger range of axial rotation compared to walking."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WALKING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A basic motor task used as a reference for comparing the range of motion in other activities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MID-SQUATS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A type of squatting activity that requires a larger range of knee flexion compared to walking."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DEEP SQUATS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A type of squatting activity that requires a larger range of knee flexion compared to walking."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LUNGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An activity that involves stepping forward and lowering the body, requiring a significant range of knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AMA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The American Medical Association, which also suggests a clinical guideline of 110 degrees of knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELECTROGONIOMETER MOTION-TRACKING DEVICE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A device used by Hemmerich and colleagues to determine the range of motion needed for various activities in healthy Indian subjects."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OPTOTRACK CERTUS 3020&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A three-dimensional tracking system used by Zhou and associates to measure the ROM during a typical kneeling posture in healthy Chinese subjects."&lt;SEP&gt;"A three-dimensional tracking system used to measure the range of motion in the hip, knee, and ankle joints."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The inward rotation of the knee, measured in degrees, required for certain activities like cross-legged sitting."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LATERAL HIP ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The outward rotation of the hip, measured in degrees, required for certain activities like cross-legged sitting."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PASSIVE KNEE ROM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The full range of motion of the knee without active muscle contraction, used as a reference in studies."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PEAK CONTACT FORCE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The maximum force experienced at the knee joint during certain activities, such as squatting."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CLINICAL GUIDELINE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A recommended standard for the range of knee flexion, typically set by medical authorities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d8383f11b0f2333960882372f1f2a14</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHINESE SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A group of healthy Chinese subjects, including 15 males and 15 females aged 24 years and 10 males and 10 females aged 64 years, who participated in a study on ROM during kneeling postures."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KAPOOR AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A research group that investigated the ROM required for cross-legged sitting in the Indian population."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INDIAN POPULATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A group of 44 healthy volunteers from India who participated in a study on ROM for cross-legged sitting."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BOONE AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A research group that conducted reliability studies of active and passive range of knee motion using universal goniometers in healthy subjects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Boone and associates conducted a study on the reliability of the Universal Goniometer (UG) in measuring knee flexion and extension ROM, finding higher intratester reliability compared to intertester reliability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Boone and associates conducted reliability studies of active and passive range of knee motion in healthy subjects using universal goniometers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1c052988db1f3bec75acfc56a5c3d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RHEAULT AND COWORKERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A research group that found good intertester reliability for the universal goniometer and the fluid-based inclinometer for measurements of active knee flexion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who investigated the concurrent validity of a universal goniometer and an inclinometer for measuring active knee flexion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rheault and coworkers found good intertester reliability for the UG in measuring active knee flexion, but noted significant differences in ROM values between the UG and fluid-based inclinometer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rheault and coworkers investigated the concurrent validity of a UG and an inclinometer for active knee flexion measurements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-907485f01764ad42a85685e6e4a21163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FLUID-BASED INCLINOMETER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A device used to measure the range of motion in the knee joint, showing good reliability but significant differences in ROM values compared to other instruments."&lt;SEP&gt;"The fluid-based inclinometer is another tool used to measure joint angles, particularly the range of motion (ROM) of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BARTHOLOMY, CHANDLER, AND KAPLAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A research group that compared measurements of passive knee flexion ROM taken with a universal goniometer, a fluid-based inclinometer, and an Optotrak motion-analysis system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bartholomy, Chandler, and Kaplan compared the UG, fluid-based inclinometer, and Optotrak motion analysis system for measuring passive knee flexion ROM, finding each reliable but not interchangeable."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OPTOTRAK MOTION-ANALYSIS SYSTEM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A system used to measure the range of motion in the knee joint, showing significant differences in ROM values compared to other instruments."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BROSSEAU AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A research group that used a universal goniometer and a parallel goniometer to measure flexion-angle positions in the right knees of healthy subjects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Brosseau and associates used the UG and parallel goniometer (PG) to measure knee flexion angles, finding good to excellent intratester reliability and lower intertester reliability for smaller angles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PARALLEL GONIOMETER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A tool used to measure the range of motion in the knee joint, showing good intratester reliability but lower intertester reliability for smaller angles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PETERS AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A research group that compared the use of a universal goniometer by physical therapists with visual estimation by physicians and radiographic goniometry measurements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RADIOGRAPHIC GONIOMETRY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A method involving the use of electronic medical imaging to measure the range of motion in the knee joint, providing precise angle measurements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE ARTHROPLASTY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Clinical guidelines for knee replacement surgery, which recommend a certain range of motion for optimal outcomes."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EASTERN AND ASIAN CULTURES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cultures where cross-legged sitting is a common posture, important for activities of daily living."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEALTHY SUBJECTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A general term referring to individuals without known health issues who participate in various studies."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PATIENT POPULATIONS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Groups of individuals with specific health conditions who are studied for medical research."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ACTIVE RANGE OF MOTION (AROM)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Active Range of Motion (AROM) is the range of movement a joint can achieve without assistance, typically measured during voluntary movement."&lt;SEP&gt;"The range of movement a joint can achieve with the help of muscles, typically measured in clinical settings."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PASSIVE RANGE OF MOTION (PROM)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Passive Range of Motion (PROM) is the range of movement a joint can achieve with assistance, typically measured when the muscle is relaxed."&lt;SEP&gt;"The range of movement a joint can achieve without muscle contraction, typically measured in clinical settings."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTRATESTER RELIABILITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Intratester Reliability refers to the consistency of measurements when performed by the same tester across multiple sessions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The consistency of measurements taken by a single tester over multiple trials."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERTESTER RELIABILITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Intertester Reliability refers to the consistency of measurements taken by different testers, indicating the reliability of the measurement tool."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intertester Reliability refers to the consistency of measurements when performed by different testers."&lt;SEP&gt;"The consistency of measurements taken by different testers on the same subject."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTRACLASS CORRELATION COEFFICIENT (ICC)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A statistical measure used to assess the reliability of measurements, particularly in reliability studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (ICC) is a statistical measure used to assess the reliability of measurements, particularly in studies involving repeated measures."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PEARSON PRODUCT-MOMENT CORRELATION COEFFICIENT (R)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A statistical measure used to assess the linear relationship between two variables."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PICTURE ARCHIVE COMMUNICATION SYSTEM (PACS)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A system used for storing and accessing medical images, often used in radiographic goniometry."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9b1b821493096bdf55f2fad5cca454b3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VISUAL ESTIMATION (VE)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A method of estimating angles without the use of a measuring tool, often less reliable than goniometric measurements."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROTHSTEIN, MILLER, AND ROETTGER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Researchers who conducted a study on the reliability of the Universal Goniometer in patient populations with knee problems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rothstein, Miller, and Roettger investigated intratester, intertester, and interdevice reliability in a study involving patients referred to physical therapy for knee issues, finding high intratester and interdevice reliability."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WATKINS AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Researchers who compared passive ROM measurements of the knees using the Universal Goniometer and Visual Estimation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Watkins and associates compared passive ROM measurements of the knees of patients using the UG and visual estimates, finding high intratester reliability for the UG and suggesting against using visual estimates due to additional errors."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PANDYA AND COLLEAGUES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Pandya and colleagues studied intratester and intertester reliability of passive knee extension measurements in children with Duchenne muscular dystrophy, finding high intratester reliability but only fair intertester reliability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who studied the reliability of passive knee extension measurements in children with Duchenne muscular dystrophy."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MCWHIRK AND GLANZMAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"McWhirk and Glanzman conducted a study on the intertester reliability of knee ROM and popliteal angle measurements in children with spastic cerebral palsy."&lt;SEP&gt;"McWhirk and Glanzman had two physical therapists measure the knee ROM and popliteal angle in children with spastic cerebral palsy, finding an intertester reliability of 0.78 for knee extension measurements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who measured knee ROM and popliteal angle in children with spastic cerebral palsy."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950c58b7df97a4bc1fdeb2087de52f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7abf467a8b1da896b788e651526495</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LESSEN AND ASSOCIATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Lessen and associates conducted a study on the intertester reliability of knee flexion and extension measurements in patients after total knee arthroplasty."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who measured active and passive knee flexion and extension in patients after total knee arthroplasty."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7abf467a8b1da896b788e651526495</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KILGOUR, MCNAIR, AND STOTT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Kilgour, McNair, and Stott conducted a study on the intrasessional and intersessional reliability of knee extension measurements in children with spastic cerebral palsy and healthy controls."&lt;SEP&gt;"Researchers who measured bilateral knee extension in children with spastic cerebral palsy and healthy controls."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-90aa2c67eee53a88d282d9e6987ca28b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7abf467a8b1da896b788e651526495</data>
</node>
