  <data key="d2">chunk-0a7abf467a8b1da896b788e651526495</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ICC&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"ICC stands for Intraclass Correlation Coefficient, a measure of reliability and validity in the context of the studies mentioned in the text."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ENWEMEKA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A researcher who compared goniometric measurements of knee joint positions with radiographic measurements, finding small mean differences at certain angles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEALTHY ADULT VOLUNTEERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A group of individuals without known health issues, used as subjects in the study by Enwemeka."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE JOINT ANGLES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Joint Angles refer to the measurements of the angles formed by the knee joint during flexion, ranging from 0 to 120 degrees."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PENDULUM GONIOMETER (PG)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A tool used for measuring joint angles, often compared to the Universal Goniometer in terms of validity and reliability."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ACTIVE KNEE FLEXION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Active Knee Flexion refers to the movement of the knee joint initiated by the individual's muscles, typically measured in different positions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CONCURRENT VALIDITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Concurrent Validity is a type of validity that assesses the degree to which two tests or measurement tools produce similar results at the same time."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PATELLOFEMORAL COMPARTMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Patellofemoral Compartment is a part of the knee joint where the patella and femur articulate, often assessed in studies of knee osteoarthritis."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNAL ROTATION ROM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Internal Rotation ROM refers to the range of motion of the knee joint in the internal rotation direction, often used to predict the progression of knee osteoarthritis."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ADVANCED DISEASE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Advanced Disease refers to a stage of knee osteoarthritis where significant damage and functional impairment are present."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEDIAL AND LATERAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Medial and Lateral Rotation refer to the movements of the knee joint in the medial and lateral directions, often measured to assess knee function and disease progression."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d86601906259a5a5da4abf14e4637db5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LUMBAR SPINE&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
  <data key="d1">"Early lumbar rounding during squatting can indicate core weakness, as the lumbar spine compensates for the lack of core stability."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SLOW DESCENT&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Pain results in Slow Descent, a prolonged eccentric loading during stair descent."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP DROP&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Pain leads to Hip Drop, excessive contralateral hip drop indicating gluteal compensation during stair descent."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WEIGHT-BEARING SITUATION&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
  <data key="d1">"In a weight-bearing situation, the femoral condyles roll and slide in opposite directions to remain on the tibial surfaces, contributing to the function of the tibiofemoral joint."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 9.5&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 9.5 identifies bony anatomical landmarks for aligning the goniometer accurately."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;END OF KNEE FLEXION&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 9.8 depicts the end of the knee flexion ROM, with the examiner maintaining the position and aligning the goniometer."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;END-FEEL&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
  <data key="d1">"The vastus medialis can contribute to a firm end-feel during knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEEL AND BUTTOCKS&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-9465146ed06caf8469c30b5a0d8be558</data>
  <data key="d1">"The contact between the heel and buttocks can contribute to a soft end-feel during knee flexion."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POSTERIOR JOINT CAPSULE&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
  <data key="d1">"The posterior joint capsule contributes to the firm end-feel experienced during knee extension, indicating the limit of the movement."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIP NEUTRAL ABDUCTION, ADDUCTION, AND ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
  <data key="d1">"Maintaining the hip in neutral abduction, adduction, and rotation is important for accurate measurement of knee flexion ROM.</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BICEPS FEMORIS (SHORT HEAD)&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
  <data key="d1">"The short head of the biceps femoris muscle originates from the lateral lip of the linea aspera, contributing to its function."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AGE&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Flexion ROM is influenced by age, with neonates exhibiting extension limitations that resolve by early adolescence."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GENDER&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Flexion ROM is influenced by gender, with studies showing differences in mean values between men and women."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BODY MASS&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
  <data key="d1">"Knee Flexion ROM can be affected by body mass, which may influence the range of motion in the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NORMAL END-FEEL&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Semitendinosus Muscle contributes to the firm end-feel experienced during the Distal Hamstring Length Test."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHILDREN&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
  <data key="d1">"Boone noted a mean of 5.4 degrees of extension beyond 0 degrees in children between 1 and 5 years of age, indicating the normal range of hyperextension in young children."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;JOINT LAXITY&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
  <data key="d1">"Beighton, Solomon, and Soskolne studied joint laxity in 1,081 individuals, finding that joint laxity decreases rapidly throughout childhood and more slowly in adulthood."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELDERLY&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
  <data key="d1">"Mollinger and Steffan assessed knee ROM in nursing home residents, finding that only 13% had full passive knee extension and 42% had greater than 10 degrees of extension limitations."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELDERLY WITH OSTEOARTHRITIS&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
  <data key="d1">"Ersoz and Ergun studied 44- to 76-year-old patients with primary knee osteoarthritis, finding that 82.5% of the knees tested had extension limitations ranging from 1 to 14 degrees."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXTENSION LIMITATIONS&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
  <data key="d1">"The hip joint, similar to the knee joint, can experience extension limitations, which are part of the broader category of joint mobility issues."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RESEARCH STUDIES&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
  <data key="d1">"Standard deviation (SD) is a statistical measure used in research studies to describe the variability of data, such as knee ROM measurements."</data>
  <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KNEE ROM MEASUREMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d2">chunk-186755fd221aeca3c68ef3cd832cff76</data>
