  <data key="d4">"The knee joint can perform rotational movements, including medial and lateral rotation, which are important for its overall function."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint movement, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;PASSIVE RANGE OF MOTION (PROM)&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The knee's passive range of motion is the range of movement that can be achieved with external assistance, often used to assess joint flexibility."</data>
  <data key="d5">"medical assessment, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;VOLUNTARY ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The knee can perform voluntary rotation, which is an active movement controlled by the individual, typically at 90 degrees of flexion."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint movement, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;ARTHROKINEMATICS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Arthrokinematics describes the movement of the knee joint surfaces, including the sliding and rolling of the femoral and tibial condyles during flexion and extension."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint mechanics, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;NON-WEIGHT-BEARING ACTIVE MOTION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Non-weight-bearing active motion of the knee involves movements performed by the individual without the weight of the body acting on the joint, helping to assess muscle control and joint function."</data>
  <data key="d5">"medical assessment, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;WEIGHT-BEARING FLEXION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Weight-bearing flexion of the knee involves bending the joint while supporting the weight of the body, which is important for functional activities."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint movement, functional assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;WEIGHT-BEARING EXTENSION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Weight-bearing extension of the knee involves straightening the joint while supporting the weight of the body, which is important for functional activities."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint movement, functional assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;MENISCI&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The menisci are important structures within the knee joint that provide cushioning and stability, helping to distribute forces and reduce friction."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint structure, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;PATELLA&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The patella, or kneecap, is a triangular bone that plays a crucial role in the knee joint, improving the leverage of the quadriceps muscle and protecting the joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint structure, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;GREATER TROCHANTER&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The greater trochanter is a bony landmark on the femur used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical reference, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;LATERAL EPICONDYLE OF THE FEMUR&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The lateral epicondyle of the femur is a bony landmark on the outer side of the knee used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical reference, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;LATERAL MIDLINE OF THE FEMUR&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The lateral midline of the femur is a line along the outer side of the thigh bone used as a reference for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical reference, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;LATERAL MIDLINE OF THE FIBULA&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The lateral midline of the fibula is a line along the outer side of the lower leg bone used as a reference for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical reference, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;LATERAL MALLEOLUS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The lateral malleolus is a bony landmark on the outer side of the ankle used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical reference, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;FIBULAR HEAD&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The fibular head is a bony landmark at the upper end of the fibula used as a reference point for aligning the goniometer during knee measurements."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical reference, medical assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;SEMIMEMBRANOSUS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The semimembranosus is a muscle in the posterior thigh that contributes to knee flexion and helps stabilize the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;SEMITENDINOSUS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The semitendinosus is a muscle in the posterior thigh that contributes to knee flexion and helps stabilize the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;BICEPS FEMORIS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The biceps femoris is a muscle in the posterior thigh that contributes to knee flexion and helps stabilize the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;THIGH&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The thigh is the upper part of the leg, containing important muscles and bones such as the femur, which are crucial for knee joint function."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;LEG&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The leg is the lower part of the limb, containing important bones such as the tibia and fibula, which are crucial for knee joint function."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;FEMUR&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The femur is the longest and strongest bone in the human body, located in the thigh and forming part of the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;TIBIA&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibia is the larger and stronger of the two bones in the lower leg, forming part of the knee joint and extending to the ankle."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;FEMORAL CONDYLES&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The femoral condyles are the rounded ends of the femur that articulate with the tibia to form the knee joint, allowing for movements such as flexion and extension."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;TIBIAL CONDYLES&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibial condyles are the upper surfaces of the tibia that articulate with the femoral condyles to form the knee joint, allowing for movements such as flexion and extension."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibiofemoral joint is the joint formed by the articulation of the tibia and femur, allowing for movements such as flexion and extension of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;MEDIAL FEMORAL CONDYLE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The medial femoral condyle is the inner rounded end of the femur that articulates with the tibia to form part of the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;LATERAL FEMORAL CONDYLE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The lateral femoral condyle is the outer rounded end of the femur that articulates with the tibia to form part of the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;NORMAL END-FEEL&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The normal end-feel is the sensation experienced by the examiner when the knee reaches the end of its range of motion, typically characterized by firm resistance due to muscle tension."</data>
  <data key="d5">"medical assessment, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;STABILIZATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Stabilization is the process of keeping the femur stable during range of motion testing to ensure accurate measurement and prevent unwanted movements."</data>
  <data key="d5">"medical procedure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;STARTING POSITION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The starting position is the initial posture or alignment of the body and limbs before performing a range of motion test, ensuring consistency and accuracy in the measurement."</data>
  <data key="d5">"medical procedure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6854c72d0616470d858bc189ee907e36</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ANKLE&quot;" target="&quot;ACHILLES TIGHTNESS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Limited dorsiflexion in the ankle can indicate Achilles tightness, affecting gait and movement.</data>
  <data key="d5">"movement limitation, muscle flexibility"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ANKLE&quot;" target="&quot;POSTERIOR IMPINGEMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Pain during plantarflexion can indicate posterior impingement, a condition where there is bony or soft tissue impingement in the ankle.</data>
  <data key="d5">"pain, joint condition"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ANKLE&quot;" target="&quot;ANKLE SPRAINS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Limited inversion movements in the ankle can indicate ankle sprains, affecting stability and movement.</data>
  <data key="d5">"movement limitation, joint injury"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ANKLE&quot;" target="&quot;PERONEAL TENDON DYSFUNCTION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Reduced eversion movements in the ankle can indicate peroneal tendon dysfunction, affecting stability and movement.</data>
  <data key="d5">"movement limitation, joint condition"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;AI-BASED COMPENSATION DETECTION RULES&quot;" target="&quot;GAIT ANALYSIS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Gait Analysis is a component of the AI-Based Compensation Detection Rules, used to identify compensations or abnormalities in walking patterns."</data>
  <data key="d5">"walking pattern analysis, compensation detection"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;AI-BASED COMPENSATION DETECTION RULES&quot;" target="&quot;KNEE PAIN COMPENSATION DETECTION RULES&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain Compensation Detection Rules are a subset of the AI-Based Compensation Detection Rules, focusing on detecting compensations due to knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d5">"subset, knee pain compensation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GAIT ANALYSIS&quot;" target="&quot;ANTALGIC GAIT&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Gait analysis can identify an antalgic gait, characterized by reduced stance phase on the affected leg due to knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d5">"gait pattern, pain compensation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GAIT ANALYSIS&quot;" target="&quot;HIP HIKE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Gait analysis can identify a hip hike, where the pelvis on the affected side is excessively elevated to compensate for knee stiffness."</data>
  <data key="d5">"gait pattern, compensation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GAIT ANALYSIS&quot;" target="&quot;FOOT DRAGGING&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Gait analysis can identify foot dragging, where there is decreased dorsiflexion due to knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d5">"gait pattern, movement limitation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GAIT ANALYSIS&quot;" target="&quot;CIRCUMDUCTION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Gait analysis can identify circumduction, where the leg swings outward to avoid bending the knee.</data>
  <data key="d5">"gait pattern, movement compensation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GAIT ANALYSIS&quot;" target="&quot;REDUCED STEP LENGTH&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Gait analysis can identify reduced step length, where the step length on the affected side is shorter due to knee pain."</data>
  <data key="d5">"gait pattern, movement limitation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GAIT ANALYSIS&quot;" target="&quot;INCREASED HIP EXTERNAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Gait analysis can identify increased hip external rotation, where the hip externally rotates to offload knee flexion stress.</data>
  <data key="d5">"gait pattern, movement compensation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SCAPULA&quot;" target="&quot;ROTATOR CUFF WEAKNESS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The scapula moves abnormally early during shoulder flexion if there is rotator cuff weakness, indicating a compensation pattern."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensation, muscle weakness"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CORE WEAKNESS&quot;" target="&quot;LUMBAR SPINE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Early lumbar rounding during squatting can indicate core weakness, as the lumbar spine compensates for the lack of core stability."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensation, core stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIGHT HAMSTRINGS&quot;" target="&quot;LUMBAR SPINE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Early lumbar rounding during squatting can also indicate tight hamstrings, as the lumbar spine compensates for the lack of flexibility in the hamstrings."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensation, muscle flexibility"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ANTALGIC GAIT&quot;" target="&quot;BACK PAIN (LUMBAR AND THORACIC)&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Back Pain (Lumbar and Thoracic) results in Antalgic Gait, a shorter stride to avoid lumbar compression."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INCREASED HIP EXTERNAL ROTATION&quot;" target="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain causes Increased Hip External Rotation, a movement to offload knee flexion stress."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;REDUCED KNEE FLEXION&quot;" target="&quot;SQUAT &amp; SIT-TO-STAND MOVEMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"During squatting and sit-to-stand movements, reduced knee flexion can indicate knee pain or injury, preventing the individual from reaching normal depth.</data>
  <data key="d5">"movement limitation, pain compensation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;REDUCED KNEE FLEXION&quot;" target="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain results in Reduced Knee Flexion, the inability to reach normal depth during squatting."</data>
  <data key="d5">"activity limitation, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FORWARD TRUNK LEAN&quot;" target="&quot;SQUAT &amp; SIT-TO-STAND MOVEMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"During squatting and sit-to-stand movements, forward trunk lean can indicate an attempt to reduce knee demand by increasing hip flexion.</data>
  <data key="d5">"movement compensation, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FORWARD TRUNK LEAN&quot;" target="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain causes Forward Trunk Lean, an increased hip flexion to reduce knee demand during squatting."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;VALGUS COLLAPSE&quot;" target="&quot;SQUAT &amp; SIT-TO-STAND MOVEMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"During squatting and sit-to-stand movements, valgus collapse can indicate medial knee collapse, often due to weak glute medius.</data>
  <data key="d5">"movement compensation, muscle strength"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7f7944fa53271cc7aa6b58f4001aaa11</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;VALGUS COLLAPSE&quot;" target="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain leads to Valgus Collapse, the medial displacement of the knee due to weak glutes or pain avoidance."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CYNTHIA C. NORKIN, PT, EDD&quot;" target="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Cynthia C. Norkin, PT, EdD, is the author of the chapter on the structure and function of the knee, providing detailed anatomical and functional information."</data>
  <data key="d5">"authorship, expertise"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The Knee chapter discusses the tibiofemoral joint as one of the two main articulations of the knee, detailing its structure and function."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical component, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;PATELLOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The Knee chapter discusses the patellofemoral joint as the second main articulation of the knee, detailing its structure and function."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical component, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;GONIOMETER&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The Knee's range of motion is measured using a goniometer, which is essential for assessing its function and identifying any limitations."</data>
  <data key="d5">"measurement, assessment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;KNEE EXTENSION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Extension is a specific aspect of the knee's range of motion, particularly important in understanding the development and health of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d5">"development, health"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;SEMIMEMBRANOSUS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The Semimembranosus muscle contributes to the firmness of the knee, affecting its function and range of motion."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle function, knee firmness"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;SEMITENDINOSUS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The Semitendinosus muscle contributes to the firmness of the knee, affecting its function and range of motion."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle function, knee firmness"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;BICEPS FEMORIS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The Biceps Femoris muscle contributes to the firmness of the knee, affecting its function and range of motion."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle function, knee firmness"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THE KNEE&quot;" target="&quot;HAMSTRING MUSCLES&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The Hamstring Muscles, including the semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and biceps femoris, are crucial for the function and range of motion of the knee."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle function, knee firmness"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-5ef81e2a2ef718984fd40ce92806308f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;FEMUR&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibiofemoral joint involves the femur, which forms the upper part of the joint with its medial and lateral condyles."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical connection, joint formation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;TIBIA&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibiofemoral joint involves the tibia, which forms the lower part of the joint with its medial and lateral condyles."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical connection, joint formation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;MENISCI&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibiofemoral joint includes the menisci, which are joint discs attached to the tibial condyles, providing cushioning and stability."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical component, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;INTERCONDYLAR NOTCH&quot;">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibiofemoral joint features the intercondylar notch, a deep groove between the medial and lateral condyles of the femur."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical feature, joint structure"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;INTERCONDYLAR TUBERCLES&quot;">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibiofemoral joint includes the intercondylar tubercles, bony spines that separate the medial and lateral condyles of the tibia."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical feature, joint structure"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;FEMORAL PATELLAR SURFACE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibiofemoral joint includes the femoral patellar surface, the area on the anterior part of the femur where it articulates with the patella."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical feature, joint structure"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;ANTERIOR CRUCIATE LIGAMENT (ACL)&quot;">
  <data key="d3">10.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The ACL is a crucial stabilizer of the tibiofemoral joint, preventing anterior displacement of the tibia relative to the femur."</data>
  <data key="d5">"ligament function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;POSTERIOR CRUCIATE LIGAMENT (PCL)&quot;">
  <data key="d3">10.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The PCL is a crucial stabilizer of the tibiofemoral joint, preventing posterior displacement of the tibia relative to the femur."</data>
  <data key="d5">"ligament function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;MEDIAL COLLATERAL LIGAMENT (MCL)&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The MCL provides medial stability to the tibiofemoral joint, preventing excessive medial movement."</data>
  <data key="d5">"ligament function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;LATERAL COLLATERAL LIGAMENT (LCL)&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The LCL provides lateral stability to the tibiofemoral joint, preventing excessive lateral movement."</data>
  <data key="d5">"ligament function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;ILIOTIBIAL BAND (ITB)&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The ITB provides lateral stability to the tibiofemoral joint, helping to prevent excessive lateral movement."</data>
  <data key="d5">"ligament function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;PES ANSERINUS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The pes anserinus provides medial stability to the tibiofemoral joint, helping to prevent excessive medial movement."</data>
  <data key="d5">"ligament function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;SCREW-HOME MECHANISM&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The screw-home mechanism is an automatic rotation that occurs during the last 10 to 15 degrees of knee extension, where the tibia laterally rotates to lock the knee in the fully extended position."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint function, automatic rotation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;CAPSULAR PATTERN&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The capsular pattern at the knee is characterized by a smaller limitation of extension compared to flexion, with no restriction of rotations, and can be associated with conditions like arthritis."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint condition, mobility"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;NON-WEIGHT-BEARING ACTIVE MOTION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Non-weight-bearing active motion involves the sliding of the tibial condyles on the femoral condyles, contributing to the function of the tibiofemoral joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint function, movement mechanics"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;WEIGHT-BEARING SITUATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"In a weight-bearing situation, the femoral condyles roll and slide in opposite directions to remain on the tibial surfaces, contributing to the function of the tibiofemoral joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint function, movement mechanics"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;AUTOMATIC ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Automatic rotation is an involuntary movement of the tibia relative to the femur, occurring primarily toward the end of knee extension to lock the knee."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint function, automatic movement"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;VOLUNTARY ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Voluntary rotation is a controlled movement of the tibia relative to the femur, possible when the knee is flexed."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint function, controlled movement"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Internal rotation is a type of voluntary rotation where the tibia rotates medially relative to the femur, contributing to the function of the tibiofemoral joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint function, controlled movement"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;EXTERNAL ROTATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"External rotation is a type of voluntary rotation where the tibia rotates laterally relative to the femur, contributing to the function of the tibiofemoral joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"joint function, controlled movement"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PATELLOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;PATELLA&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The patellofemoral joint involves the patella, which articulates with the femoral patellar surface of the femur."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical connection, joint formation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PATELLOFEMORAL JOINT&quot;" target="&quot;FEMORAL PATELLAR SURFACE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">18.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The femoral patellar surface is the area where the patella articulates with the femur, forming the patellofemoral joint."&lt;SEP&gt;"The patellofemoral joint involves the femoral patellar surface, the area on the anterior part of the femur where it articulates with the patella."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical connection, joint formation"&lt;SEP&gt;"anatomical structure, articulation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MENISCI&quot;" target="&quot;TIBIAL CONDYLES&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The menisci are attached to the tibial condyles, providing cushioning and stability to the tibiofemoral joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, attachment"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERCONDYLAR NOTCH&quot;" target="&quot;MEDIAL CONDYLE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The medial condyle is separated from the lateral condyle by the intercondylar notch, which is a deep groove on the femur."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, separation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERCONDYLAR TUBERCLES&quot;" target="&quot;TIBIAL CONDYLES&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibial condyles are separated by the intercondylar tubercles, which are bony spines on the tibia."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, separation"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-efbffe4c52beae140acaca62d94996bd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIA&quot;" target="&quot;FEMUR&quot;">
  <data key="d3">10.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibia and femur form the tibiofemoral joint, interacting through their condyles to allow flexion-extension and rotation movements."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIA&quot;" target="&quot;PATELLA&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The tibia interacts with the patella via the patellar ligament, which connects the patella to the tibial tuberosity."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIA&quot;" target="&quot;LATERAL MENISCUS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The lateral meniscus sits on the lateral condyle of the tibia, providing cushioning and stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"meniscus function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TIBIA&quot;" target="&quot;MEDIAL MENISCUS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The medial meniscus sits on the medial condyle of the tibia, providing cushioning and stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"meniscus function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FEMUR&quot;" target="&quot;PATELLA&quot;">
  <data key="d3">10.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The femur and patella form the patellofemoral joint, where the patella articulates with the femoral patellar surface."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomical structure, joint function"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FEMUR&quot;" target="&quot;POPLITEAL ANGLE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The Femur is one of the bones forming the popliteal angle, which is measured during the Distal Hamstring Length Test."</data>
  <data key="d5">"anatomy, measurement"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b877890fad5a011b1a06d5c0f204fb41</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PATELLA&quot;" target="&quot;QUADRICEPS TENDON&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The quadriceps tendon connects the quadriceps muscle to the patella, providing anterior stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle-tendon interaction, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PATELLA&quot;" target="&quot;PATELLAR LIGAMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The patellar ligament is a continuation of the quadriceps tendon, connecting the patella to the tibial tuberosity and providing anterior stability to the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"ligament function, joint stability"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d18e09b8ab3ad7a43570ad9f8c83a2fd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PATELLA&quot;" target="&quot;QUADRICEPS FEMORIS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"The quadriceps femoris muscles merge into the quadriceps tendon, which inserts into the patella.</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle insertion, anatomical landmark"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c5c8b5077967f8035788e5f388ddf3c9</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PATELLA&quot;" target="&quot;ELY TEST&quot;">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"During the Ely test, a small towel roll may be placed under the distal femur to reduce pressure on the patella, ensuring comfort and accuracy."</data>
  <data key="d5">"comfort, pressure reduction"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0aebf522eb13809f43facec9b7c1f746</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;CIRCUMDUCTION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain leads to Circumduction, an outward swinging of the leg to avoid bending the knee."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;DECREASED STEP LENGTH&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain results in Decreased Step Length, a shorter step on the affected side to reduce knee stress."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;SQUAT &amp; SIT-TO-STAND MOVEMENTS&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain affects Squat &amp; Sit-to-Stand Movements, leading to reduced knee flexion and forward trunk lean."</data>
  <data key="d5">"activity limitation, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;HEEL ELEVATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain results in Heel Elevation, lifting the heels to avoid loading the quadriceps."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;UNEVEN WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain causes Uneven Weight Distribution, placing more weight on the non-affected leg to avoid loading the painful knee."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;STAIRS (ASCENDING &amp; DESCENDING)&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain affects Stairs (Ascending &amp; Descending), leading to leading with the non-affected leg and slow descent."</data>
  <data key="d5">"activity limitation, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;LEADING WITH NON-AFFECTED LEG&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain causes Leading with Non-Affected Leg, a strategy to avoid loading the painful knee during stair climbing."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;SLOW DESCENT&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain results in Slow Descent, a prolonged eccentric loading during stair descent."</data>
  <data key="d5">"activity limitation, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;HIP DROP&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain leads to Hip Drop, excessive contralateral hip drop indicating gluteal compensation during stair descent."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;RANGE OF MOTION (ROM) DEFICIENCIES&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain results in Range of Motion (ROM) Deficiencies, limitations in the movement of the knee joint."</data>
  <data key="d5">"activity limitation, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;PAIN AT END-RANGE MOTION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain causes Pain at End-Range Motion, limited movement due to discomfort at the extremes of knee motion."</data>
  <data key="d5">"activity limitation, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;JOINT LOADING &amp; ASYMMETRY&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain leads to Joint Loading &amp; Asymmetry, the uneven distribution of forces across joints."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;INCREASED HIP AND ANKLE LOAD&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain results in Increased Hip and Ankle Load, a compensatory mechanism to avoid knee motion."</data>
  <data key="d5">"compensatory movement, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;KNEE PAIN&quot;" target="&quot;DECREASED QUADRICEPS ACTIVATION&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"Knee Pain causes Decreased Quadriceps Activation, reduced knee extension force."</data>
  <data key="d5">"muscle function, pain avoidance"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-976ab4610243e0d2d12cedea97482ec4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TENNIS ELBOW (LATERAL EPICONDYLITIS)&quot;" target="&quot;REDUCED WRIST EXTENSION STRENGTH&quot;">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
